(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-nephrology-urinary-quiz-version-5"]={"title":"Nephrology & Urinary System — Anatomy Exam 3 (Set 5)","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"The right kidney typically spans approximately which vertebral levels?","choices":["T10 to L1","L2 to L5","T8 to T11","T12 to L3"],"answer":3,"exp":"The right kidney typically spans approximately T12 to L3. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"The left kidney typically spans approximately which vertebral levels?","choices":["T12 to L3","L1 to L4","T10/11 to L2","T8 to T11"],"answer":2,"exp":"The left kidney typically spans approximately T10/11 to L2, slightly more superior than the right kidney. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"The average length of an adult kidney is approximately:","choices":["16 cm","20 cm","6 cm","11 cm"],"answer":3,"exp":"The average adult kidney is approximately 11 cm in length. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"On which side does the renal vein additionally receive the gonadal vein before draining to the IVC?","choices":["Right side only","Left side only","Both sides equally","Neither side"],"answer":1,"exp":"On the left side, the renal vein additionally receives the gonadal vein before draining toward the IVC. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"According to the lecture, each kidney typically contains approximately how many renal pyramids?","choices":["8 to 15","30 to 40","2 to 4","20 to 25"],"answer":0,"exp":"Each kidney typically contains approximately 8 to 15 renal pyramids. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"The apex of each renal pyramid, which points toward the renal sinus, is called the:","choices":["Renal papilla","Renal column","Renal arch","Renal hilum"],"answer":0,"exp":"The apex of each renal pyramid is called the renal papilla. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"The base of each renal pyramid lies against which structure?","choices":["The renal capsule directly","The renal cortex","The renal sinus","The renal pelvis"],"answer":1,"exp":"The base of each renal pyramid lies against the renal cortex. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which layer surrounding the kidney is described as completely surrounding it and providing cushioning and insulation?","choices":["Paranephric fat outside the renal fascia","Fibrous (renal) capsule on the cortex","Perinephric fat (adipose capsule)","Renal fascia anchoring the kidney"],"answer":2,"exp":"The perinephric fat (adipose capsule) completely surrounds the kidney, providing cushioning and insulation. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which layer surrounding the kidney anchors it to the posterior abdominal wall and peritoneum?","choices":["Paranephric fat","Perinephric fat","Fibrous (renal) capsule","Renal fascia"],"answer":3,"exp":"The renal fascia is dense irregular connective tissue that anchors the kidney to the posterior wall and peritoneum. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Paranephric (pararenal) fat is located between which two structures?","choices":["The cortex and medulla","The renal sinus and renal pelvis","The renal fascia and peritoneum","The renal capsule and perinephric fat"],"answer":2,"exp":"Paranephric fat lies between the renal fascia and the peritoneum, external to the other kidney-surrounding layers. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Arterial blood destined for the glomerulus travels: aorta, renal artery, segmental arteries, lobar arteries, interlobar arteries, arcuate artery, interlobular artery, then which vessel?","choices":["Vasa recta","Afferent arteriole","Peritubular capillary","Efferent arteriole"],"answer":1,"exp":"After the interlobular artery, blood travels through the afferent arteriole before reaching the glomerulus. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Venous blood draining the kidney travels: vasa recta, interlobular vein, arcuate vein, interlobar vein, then which vessel, before the IVC?","choices":["Renal vein","Gonadal vein","Segmental vein","Lobar vein"],"answer":0,"exp":"After the interlobar vein, blood drains into the renal vein before reaching the inferior vena cava. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"The efferent arterioles branch into peritubular capillaries or which other capillary network?","choices":["Interlobular capillaries","Arcuate capillaries","Vasa recta","Glomerular capillaries"],"answer":2,"exp":"The efferent arterioles branch into either peritubular capillaries or the vasa recta. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"The renal plexus, which innervates the kidney, accompanies which vessel and enters through the hilum?","choices":["The renal vein","The ureter","The renal artery","The gonadal artery"],"answer":2,"exp":"The renal plexus accompanies the renal artery and enters the kidney through the hilum. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Greater splanchnic nerve fibers to the kidney arise from which spinal nerve roots?","choices":["T5–T9","T1–T4","T10–T11","T12 only"],"answer":0,"exp":"The greater splanchnic nerves supplying the kidney arise from T5–T9 nerve roots. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Lesser splanchnic nerve fibers to the kidney arise from which spinal nerve roots?","choices":["T12","T5–T9","L1–L2","T10–T11"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lesser splanchnic nerves supplying the kidney arise from T10–T11 nerve roots. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"The least splanchnic nerve, arising from the T12 root, is described as serving mainly which function for the kidney?","choices":["Parasympathetic secretomotor function","Sensory innervation","Innervation of the adrenal medulla only","Motor innervation of the ureter"],"answer":1,"exp":"The least splanchnic nerve (T12) is described as mainly sensory for the kidney. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"According to the lecture's receptor terminology, 'adrenergic' receptors correspond to which division of the autonomic nervous system?","choices":["Somatic","Parasympathetic","Enteric","Sympathetic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Adrenergic receptors correspond to the sympathetic division of the autonomic nervous system. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"According to the lecture's receptor terminology, 'cholinergic' and 'muscarinic' receptors correspond to which division of the autonomic nervous system?","choices":["Somatic motor","Parasympathetic","Sympathetic","None (they are unrelated to the ANS)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cholinergic and muscarinic receptors correspond to the parasympathetic division of the autonomic nervous system. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"The renal corpuscle is composed of the glomerulus and which capsule?","choices":["Bowman's (glomerular) capsule","The renal capsule itself","The adipose capsule layer","The perinephric capsule"],"answer":0,"exp":"The renal corpuscle is composed of the glomerulus and the glomerular (Bowman's) capsule. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which renal tubule segment is lined by simple cuboidal epithelium and begins at the tubular pole of the renal corpuscle?","choices":["The distal convoluted tubule","The thin descending limb","The collecting duct itself","The proximal convoluted tubule"],"answer":3,"exp":"The proximal convoluted tubule begins at the tubular pole of the renal corpuscle and is lined by simple cuboidal epithelium. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Juxtamedullary (medullary) nephrons make up approximately what percentage of all nephrons?","choices":["85%","50%","5%","15%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Juxtamedullary nephrons make up approximately 15% of all nephrons. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which type of nephron is located near the peripheral edge of the cortex and has short nephron loops?","choices":["Juxtamedullary nephrons","Juxtaglomerular nephrons","Cortical nephrons","Medullary nephrons"],"answer":2,"exp":"Cortical nephrons, located near the peripheral edge of the cortex, have short nephron loops. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which capillary network is specifically associated with juxtamedullary (medullary) nephrons rather than cortical nephrons?","choices":["Arcuate capillaries","Peritubular capillaries","Glomerular capillaries","Vasa recta"],"answer":3,"exp":"Juxtamedullary nephrons have vasa recta, in contrast to the peritubular capillaries associated with cortical nephrons. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"The fenestrated glomerular endothelium is characterized by large filtration pores that make the cells:","choices":["Highly permeable","Permeable only to gases","Selectively permeable to lipids only","Impermeable"],"answer":0,"exp":"The fenestrations (large pores) of the glomerular endothelium make the cells highly permeable. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Podocytes of the glomerulus are coated in a negatively charged substance called:","choices":["Glycocalyx","Mucin","Surfactant","Keratin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Podocytes are coated in a negatively charged substance called glycocalyx. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"The glomerular basement membrane is located between the capillary and which cells?","choices":["Macula densa cells","Juxtaglomerular cells","Mesangial cells","Podocytes"],"answer":3,"exp":"The glomerular basement membrane lies between the capillary and the podocytes. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Filtration slits of the glomerulus, made by podocyte pedicles, allow water, electrolytes, glucose, fatty acids, amino acids, and nitrogenous waste to pass while preventing the absorption of:","choices":["Small cations","Water molecules","Oxygen and carbon dioxide","Large anions and proteins"],"answer":3,"exp":"The negatively charged filtration slits prevent large anions and proteins from passing through. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"The parietal layer of the glomerular (Bowman's) capsule is composed of which epithelium?","choices":["Simple columnar epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium","Simple squamous epithelium","Transitional epithelium"],"answer":2,"exp":"The parietal layer of the glomerular capsule is composed of simple squamous epithelium. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"The space enclosed by the parietal and visceral layers of the glomerular capsule, where initial filtrate collects, is called the:","choices":["Renal sinus cavity","Capsular (Bowman's) space","Perinephric fat space","Interlobular space"],"answer":1,"exp":"The capsular space (Bowman's space) is enclosed by the parietal and visceral layers of the glomerular capsule. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 35"}]};
